        "math_academy_id": "q-320811",
        "problem": "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2y=7-3x \\\\ x+1=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n| $x=$ {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n\n| |  | {{field-2}} | $-2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  | |  |  |  | |  | $7$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-1$ |  | | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $-2$ |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |  |  |\n|  | $7$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |",
        "worked_solution": "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y=7 \\\\ x+2y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
